\details{
This function orchestrates repeated calls to \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} across
multiple facet pairs and returns a consolidated bundle.

\strong{Bias/interaction} in MFRM refers to a systematic departure from
the additive model for a specific combination of facet elements
(e.g., a particular rater is unexpectedly harsh on a particular
criterion).  See \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} for the mathematical formulation.

When \code{pairs = NULL}, the function builds all 2-way combinations of
modelled facets automatically.  For a model with facets Rater,
Criterion, and Task, this yields Rater\eqn{\times}Criterion,
Rater\eqn{\times}Task, and Criterion\eqn{\times}Task.
With \code{include_person = TRUE}, Person\eqn{\times}facet pairs are prepended in
the fit's facet order. They are conditional plug-in likelihood screens, not
jointly fitted Person\eqn{\times}facet terms. A single warning records this
boundary for the batch.

The \code{summary} table aggregates results across pairs:
\itemize{
\item \code{Rows}: number of interaction cells estimated
\item \code{Points}: cells with an available bias estimate
(including bounded optima; inspect \code{BiasStatus} in the combined cells)
\item \code{ScreenAvailable}, \code{ScreenUnavailable}: cells with/without a usable
conditional screening statistic
\item \code{ScreenPositive}: count of available cells with \eqn{|t| \ge 2};
\code{NA} when no screening statistic is available, including failed pairs
\item \code{Significant}: backward-compatible alias for \code{ScreenPositive}
\item \code{MeanAbsBias}: average absolute available bias magnitude (native ability units)
\item \code{Status}: \code{available}, \code{partial}, \code{unavailable}, \code{empty}, or \code{failed};
\code{available} refers to conditional screening, not formal inference
\item \code{FormalInferenceEligible} / \code{PrimaryReportingEligible}: always \code{FALSE};
these conditional plug-in interaction statistics are screening evidence
}

Per-pair failures (e.g., insufficient data for a sparse pair) are
captured in \code{errors} rather than stopping the entire batch.
There is no collection-wide multiplicity correction. Cells and pairs reuse
the same observations and fitted calibration. Adjusting their screening tail
areas would not establish formal family-wise error or false-discovery control.
A screen-positive result is a prompt for review, not proof of unfairness;
no screen-positive cells does not establish absence of interaction.
Search-boundary points remain visible, with missing screening statistics
and the \code{ScreenStatus}/\code{ScreenReason} returned by \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}}.

\code{summary()} method: Summarize all retained bias screens

For \code{summary()}: No model, diagnostics or cell calculation is repeated. The overview
counts finite bias estimates from successful cell optimizations separately
from cells with a finite screening statistic and positive finite conditional
SE. \code{ScreenPositive} is missing when no statistic can be screened, rather
than reporting zero. Failed pairs remain visible even when the original
batch used \code{keep_errors = FALSE}. Older collections without retained tables
are labeled \code{not_retained}; they are not recalculated.

A positive bias shift increases expected coded scores conditional on the
fitted model. It does not necessarily mean better performance or unfair
leniency. Baseline calibration and Person uncertainty are held fixed.
Different pairs use overlapping data, so do not add their observed counts
to obtain a study sample size. The overview's mean absolute bias describes
magnitude only; use the signed cell estimates to study direction.
Counts cover retained \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} cells: unobserved combinations and
cells omitted by the chosen screening options are not reconstructed.
No multiplicity-adjusted confirmatory decisions are supplied.

\code{plot()} method: Explore bias across facet pairs

For \code{plot()}: The overview plots every finite retained bias estimate, without ranking or
a significance cutoff. Positive shifts increase expected coded scores under
the fitted model; score meaning depends on the rubric. Vertical spreading
only separates cells visually and has no statistical meaning.

Filled circles have a usable conditional screening statistic; open circles
retain a point without one. These symbols indicate availability, not whether
a cell is flagged. Pair labels report usable statistics / retained cells and
the range of observed row counts per cell (\code{n}), not independent Person
counts. Pairs share observations. Empty, failed and unavailable pairs remain
labeled, without substituting a point at zero. The vertical zero line means
no conditional shift, not a threshold for practical importance.

No model or diagnostics are recalculated. The overview supplies no intervals,
multiplicity correction or formal bias test. Review the individual cell
counts, optimizer status and subject-matter explanations; selecting a pair
after this overview does not make its subsequent analysis confirmatory.
Titles and interpretation notes are returned outside the figure.
}
